Three main classes of screening rules can be distinguished:
1) Static rules {{cite:4c2da24860be7e4cedcee24b2c702cde843f5940}}, {{cite:b2e39089658915e37e2dd8fa6ffcacb43b74bb17}}, {{cite:de09c10a6fbe32f0981f8349d7642c594d853d29}} perform variable elimination once and for all prior to the optimization process;
2) Dynamic rules {{cite:0be833169a712bc32e56fc0d38b426e7e93a81c1}}, {{cite:4602ed264e37c3b308dfe7c0fda83e98c2fc7592}} perform screening repeatedly over the iterations of an iterative solver,
leveraging the improvement of the solution estimate to screen-out more coordinates;
3) Sequential rules {{cite:b2e39089658915e37e2dd8fa6ffcacb43b74bb17}}, {{cite:710095142e7653ddb85a8308bf83b8769f4b9beb}}, {{cite:13ccbce427468093e9731854242cb51b5162167c}}, {{cite:15416871708dd7f857a48e7f10335fa1bd50f6c2}}
exploit information from previously-solved problems in a regularization path approach.

