Point prediction refers to the AGCRN model which is used here to compare with other uncertainty quantification methods;

Quantile regression {{cite:b21a89da5599f9bfde69faf324f6ce093f9556e7}} is a distribution-free method which directly computes the mean, lower and upper bounds using the corresponding quantile ({{formula:1038b6c1-a691-4e0d-9219-975522f4db1e}} , {{formula:1f3eacbf-0f09-448d-a355-fd8e74cbcbbc}} , {{formula:d4a40aae-cf74-46ad-afb1-453f12ac102c}} );

Mean Variance Estimation (MVE) {{cite:b0773ed8623edf306a56170404ea6dea1965810b}} refers to the method that estimates heterogeneous aleatoric uncertainty through computing Equation (REF );

Monte Carlo dropout (MCDO) {{cite:ae85c26443c3a8dfdf0015ae5526424b23607c5c}} performs dropout at both training and test time, the number of Monte Carlo samples for inference is 10;

Combined refers to the method that calculates both epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty using Equation (REF ) {{cite:1d1882ed89ede822d19f79e0fdea16cada43e8c3}}, the number of Monte Carlo samples for inference is 10;

Temperature Scaling (TS) {{cite:80e2e5ba9ed1d3129b1c87fc2a393ad5afed5f11}} calibrates the aleatoric uncertainty obtained by MVE;

Fast Geometric Ensembling (FGE) {{cite:8cd6af580d93aca9550da44922722cde52ab1178}} performs fast ensembling via varying the learning rate, the number of the stored trained models is 10;

Locally Weighted Conformal Inference {{cite:df18db475f61a18f3f0438e64d4d0473e263bdcd}}, {{cite:ec967f2ee096eb36a0d00d3c996b5d7b1877e729}} calibrates the aleatoric uncertainty obtained by MVE via conformalization;

Conformal Forecasting Recurrent Neual Network (CFRNN) {{cite:7f05f96911ee19306fe4b5f26032be7dd27f3c78}} computes the multi-horizon uncertainty using conformal prediction;

