We compare the proposed MCLEA against 19 state-of-the-art EA methods, which can be classified into four categories:
1) structure-based methods that solely rely on structural information for aligning entities, including BootEA {{cite:a64444b6c64dabe920e56ce42911934226bdf27d}}, MUGNN {{cite:ae3ff338b66f78b32ea23fc7a4d57b24721d0fa3}}, KECG {{cite:dc0580d747355a3aa2904d9c56ca2ccc9408d808}}, NAEA {{cite:d7119b78ec42aa55c4f375c117e4d711ef570e83}}, and AliNet {{cite:8ae9f956fed90a7a68916f71c2f435aa0aade981}};
2) auxiliary-enhanced methods that utilize auxiliary information to improve the performance, including MultiKE {{cite:63c0d1ee6b35bfd0c0f011817e2bf5a12a48b613}}, HMAN {{cite:07c68db493691f9756c49faf046f0ad414531db6}},
RDGCN {{cite:f9a209dafb6eee989c2284430c82f7e979a57e11}}, AttrGNN {{cite:74cdb4c6ce25a91cc0650f43bbec85dd3d098edd}}, BERT-INT {{cite:a75dc1d026366df17d5703c7a3766719a424f61e}} and ERMC {{cite:3a2947e6261cc4081926cba4c1f0bf06cf84f325}};
3) multi-modal methods that combine the multi-modal features to generate entity representations, including PoE {{cite:a17f8cc2ed0a216f6d35f348ccf51a6b1a53382d}}, MMEA {{cite:ba1263cb64abcade3cf857466405d452e57caf1d}}, HMEA {{cite:c4688b7d6aec24b71d722f2418143a974c253a11}}, and EVA {{cite:1bc30a5be5ccc088c53c92c86937e1bacb611822}};
4) unsupervised methods, including RREA {{cite:979e3dc0b68bacd3deb7f3b8ebcac4079554b417}}, MRAEA {{cite:61d9a3bf1707b3d1a808d23ce8e8cc331da487b2}}, EASY {{cite:b7517e8d2e6aadcc6e69bddb5a2f0452bc4fea7a}}, and SEU {{cite:ccd84f1f0a37c869d8494107246ebb11c7768345}}.

The metrics used included:
(1) maximum Dice (maxDice), which measures the similarity between two sets of data;
(2) maximum specificity (maxSpe), which refers to the percentage of the samples that are negative and are judged as such;
(3) maximum IoU (maxIoU), which measures the overlap between two masks;
(4) S-measure {{cite:dd15c621919026d5343ed4ed15990e9d55e2c0ae}} ({{formula:7f32fdbf-10a8-48bc-9e06-a17cfabe8267}} ), which evaluates region- and object-aware structural similarity;
(5) enhanced-alignment measure {{cite:e9304b6e7e5fc9c1cd8d96746d3b2a50b407d397}} ({{formula:bcc1fde9-1abd-4abe-99db-4ebd0317c3d7}} ), which measures pixel-level matching and image-level statistics;
and (6) mean absolute error ({{formula:4d78363c-377f-4ff9-9a42-53ae8c922c37}} ), which measures the pixel-level error between the prediction and GT.

