SVM {{cite:958bb9044c5e73379840eac9f1bcfb3b82419c5b}}: It is a traditional support vector machine based model with extensive feature engineering;

AdaRNN {{cite:6abb94bc844fc128e85629f44dde194605b65ae7}}: It learns the sentence representation toward target for sentiment prediction via semantic composition over dependency tree;

AE-LSTM, and ATAE-LSTM {{cite:66f1f8e2fb5517edbc92e67063a21756f1c49b0e}}: AE-LSTM is a simple LSTM model incorporating the target embedding as input, while ATAE-LSTM extends AE-LSTM with attention;

IAN {{cite:c31db9530293e078d462d95e6b6150ff6b66bfd4}}: IAN employs two LSTMs to learn the representations of the context and the target phrase interactively;

CNN-ASP: It is a CNN-based model implemented by us which directly concatenates target representation to each word embedding;

TD-LSTM {{cite:97d114d9dc18238089dd125f74f2b732a337afcc}}: It employs two LSTMs to model the left and right contexts of the target separately, then performs predictions based on concatenated context representations;

MemNet {{cite:7708d8a7eac61bb2299c49c9860f8b3c39b5695a}}: It applies attention mechanism over the word embeddings multiple times and predicts sentiments based on the top-most sentence representations;

BILSTM-ATT-G {{cite:aca762a7a8e2b5fe478de164364a1a0079c89d16}}: It models left and right contexts using two attention-based LSTMs and introduces gates to measure the importance of left context, right context, and the entire sentence for the prediction;

RAM {{cite:193ced32e3aff11d7ff269bd73ee66626a66c3b0}}: RAM is a multi-layer architecture where each layer consists of attention-based aggregation of word features and a GRU cell to learn the sentence representation.

