seems to have succeeded in controlling the pandemic {{cite:bed12d33ac5446193ed388b61d5225438631654f}}.

It suffices to cite two old textbooks by Woods {{cite:a111698f776aad30d9e071d53c54d25339502049}}
and by Kestin {{cite:032c98e4be2ae89290507c66294a20434b4d7278}}, and a monograph by Eu {{cite:6a2842fe7616bf02dfe5a7cdcdd302b08fe2f4f8}}
where the same approach has been described in detail.

In most applications the ADEs represent processes for which there
is no underlying lattice (e.g. cosmic ray diffusion {{cite:d8e54d52bf4a803fac4d1245b4f1d98b9d1f94bf}}
or chemotactically moving cells {{cite:0a15a0fa34a69b438df9bd150a61807bfcac3daa}}).

In this paper, we implement the squirmer model numerically
using the lattice Boltzmann (LB) method{{cite:5ee7dad8247f42abe9a76481a09f341df2e9b856}},
making use of the {{cite:7c2d3c551b61e5b2a803fb17db7a63ff978dbad5}} moving boundary conditions{{cite:7c2d3c551b61e5b2a803fb17db7a63ff978dbad5}}.

